 significant recent interest. While most GCNs operate within Euclidean geometry, this approach often leads to high distortion when embedding graphs with scale-free or hierarchical structures. To address this, recent models have been developed to function in non-Euclidean spaces, particularly hyperbolic geometry. Although hyperbolic GCNs demonstrate promising results, current hyperbolic graph operations fail to strictly adhere to hyperbolic geometry, potentially limiting the space's utility and degrading model performance. This paper introduces a novel hyperbolic GCN, the Lorentzian graph convolutional network (LGCN), which ensures that learned node features rigorously conform to hyperbolic geometry. Specifically, we reconstruct key hyperbolic graph operations, such as feature transformation and non-linear activation, using their Lorentzian counterparts. Additionally, we propose an efficient neighborhood aggregation method grounded in Lorentzian distance centroids. We further demonstrate the equivalence of these proposed operations across different types of hyperbolic geometry, providing fundamental proof of their correctness. Experiments across six datasets reveal that LGCN outperforms state-of-the-art methods and exhibits lower distortion when learning representations for tree-like graphs compared to existing hyperbolic GCNs. Furthermore, we observe that replacing standard graph operations with those defined in this paper can enhance the performance of certain existing hyperbolic GCNs. The source code is available at https://github.com/ZiruiLi-THU/LGCN.
