) for disease-aware image editing represents a significant opportunity for enhancing AI applications in healthcare. This paper introduces a proof of concept for this approach. Although GANs have proven effective in creating and altering natural images, their use within the medical field remains nascent. By leveraging the CheXpert dataset, we demonstrate that StyleGAN can be trained to produce authentic chest X-rays. Drawing inspiration from the Cyclic Reverse Generator (CRG) framework, we developed an encoder capable of accurately inverting the generator for synthetic X-rays and reconstructing real images at the organ level. Through differential privacy (DP), we created a model that modifies X-rays to indicate various outcomes for pneumonia and cardiomegaly. Empirical evaluation confirms that our model can: (a) reverse the CRG encoding of CRG(Invert(code,DP_model)), (b) apply differentiations from the DP model to original X-rays (DP_model(Invert(code,DP_model)), and (c) modify specific features of the X-ray image (DP_model(generator(code))). These findings suggest that GANs hold promise for creating and editing medical imagery. Although this study focuses on chest X-rays and addresses only two conditions, the underlying technique may be applicable to other diseases and body parts. Additionally, we note that this research utilizes a differentially private model. While the authors believe this work advances medical image editing, the study is limited by the focus on chest X-rays and the exclusion of data from patients diagnosed with pneumonia or cardiomegaly. Finally, the achieved epsilon value of 20 indicates only a modest level of privacy. The code for this proof of concept is accessible at https://github.com/BerndSch/CRG-dp.

The objective of this work is to enable the application of generative adversarial networks (GANs) to chest X-ray editing. Specifically, we aim to modify these images to reflect various outcomes for pneumonia and cardiomegaly. Our methodology involves training a GAN on the CheXpert dataset, verifying its ability to reverse the encoding process for such modifications, and confirming that the alterations are visible in the final image.

